<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arad, Romania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arad is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on the Mureș river, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> until 1920. After the defeat of the Hungarian revolution of 1848-1849, thirteen Hungarian generals were executed there by the Austrians on 6 October 1849. These Martyrs of Arad are commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as national heroes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arad-romania.jpg" alt="Arad, Romania" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nick Savchenko from Kiev, Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arad_(30130671351).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
